-- title:
--   Proof of Theorem 4, p.4 — validity of the two-sided SPRT (Ville)
-- statement:
--   Let $0<q_0<t<1$, $0<\delta<\tfrac12$, $a\in(0,1]$, any depth $d$ and any side law $\nu$, and run the two-sided SPRT with $A=\log(1/\delta)$. Then:
--
--   1. for every $q\in[t,1]$, $e^{S_n}$ is a nonnegative supermartingale for $\mathcal F_n=\sigma(Y_1,\dots,Y_n)$ under i.i.d. Bernoulli$(q)$ target outcomes;
--   2. for every $q\in[t,1]$, the SPRT accepts with probability at most $\delta$ (false acceptance);
--   3. for every $q\in[0,q_0]$, the SPRT stops at the lower boundary, without a certificate, with probability at most $\delta$.
--
--   Both bounds come from Ville's inequality applied to $e^{S_n}$ and to $e^{-S_n}$. With almost-sure termination, item 3 gives completeness.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - encoding: q₀ = (b − ε)/(κp), t = b/(κp) with 0 < ε < b < κp → any reals 0 < q₀ < t < 1; reason: exactly the pairs that arise (κ = a = 1, b = t, ε = t − q₀ realise any such pair), so the same instances
--   - encoding: the SPRT's target observations Y_1, Y_2, … and F_n = σ(Y_1, …, Y_n) → the coordinates y 0, y 1, … of the i.i.d. Bernoulli(q) target tape (Measure.infinitePi of bern q) and pastFiltration n (coordinates < n); reason: the SPRT reads the target tape in order, so its i-th observation is target entry i − 1
--   - encoding: navigation and target outcomes with their stated conditional laws given the history; old transcript generated by a common kernel under all compared parameters; private randomness → side variable w ∼ ν (old data and private randomness, law free of q), independent of an i.i.d. Bernoulli(a) navigation tape and an i.i.d. Bernoulli(q) target tape; the k-th navigation attempt reads navigation entry k, the j-th target attempt reads target entry j (lawQ); reason: attempts are predictable, so each reads a fresh entry independent of everything observed; the joint law of (old data, randomness, fresh history) is the source's
-- source:
--   Q. Li, When Can Physical Evidence Survive a World-Model Update? Finite-cost re-auditing and reusable policy certificates, review revision of 3 November 2026 (STOC 2027 submission, sha256 a20c437b34115b84982339e9a6fa319cb3a66f66d9d4c474465cbb954c79093d), proof of Theorem 4, p.4 (PDF p.5), "Thus eSn is a nonnegative supermartingale, and Ville's inequality bounds false acceptance by δ. … The lower-boundary probability is at most δ."

import Mathlib
